      "children": [],
      "nodeName": "hr",
      "type": "Element",
    },
    {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "newpage",
      },
      "children": [
        {
          "attributes": {
            "id": "page-35",
          },
          "children": [],
          "nodeName": "span",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": "
",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "class": "grey",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "attributes": {
                "href": "/info/rfc2000/",
              },
              "children": [
                {
                  "textContent": "RFC 2000",
                  "type": "Text",
                },
              ],
              "nodeName": "a",
              "type": "Element",
            },
            {
              "textContent": "                   Internet Standards              February 1997",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "span",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": "


[Note: an asterisk at the end of a line indicates a change from the
previous edition of this document.]

Applicability Statements:

   IGMP -- The Internet Architecture Board intends to move towards
   general adoption of IP multicasting, as a more efficient solution
   than broadcasting for many applications.  The host interface has been
   standardized in ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1112/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1112",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": "; however, multicast-routing gateways are in
   the experimental stage and are not widely available.  An Internet
   host should support all of ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1112/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1112",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": ", except for the IGMP protocol
   itself which is optional; see ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1122/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1122",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": " for more details.  Even
   without IGMP, implementation of ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1112/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1112",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": " will provide an important
   advance: IP-layer access to local network multicast addressing.  It
   is expected that IGMP will become recommended for all hosts and
   gateways at some future date.

   SMI, MIB-II SNMP -- The Internet Architecture Board recommends that
   all IP and TCP implementations be network manageable.  At the current
   time, this implies implementation of the Internet MIB-II (",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1213/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1213",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": "),
   and at least the recommended management protocol SNMP (",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1157/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1157",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": ").

   RIP -- The Routing Information Protocol (RIP) is widely implemented
   and used in the Internet.  However, both implementors and users
   should be aware that RIP has some serious technical limitations as a
   routing protocol.  The IETF is currently devpeloping several
   candidates for a new standard "open" routing protocol with better
   properties than RIP.  The IAB urges the Internet community to track
   these developments, and to implement the new protocol when it is
   standardized; improved Internet service will result for many users.

   TP-TCP -- As OSI protocols become more widely implemented and used,
   there will be an increasing need to support interoperation with the
   TCP/IP protocols.  The Internet Engineering Task Force is formulating
   strategies for interoperation.  ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1006/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-1006",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": " provides one interoperation
   mode, in which TCP/IP is used to emulate TP0 in order to support OSI
   applications.  Hosts that wish to run OSI connection-oriented
   applications in this mode should use the procedure described in ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1006/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "RFC-",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": "
   ",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "href": "/info/rfc1006/",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "1006",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "a",
          "type": "Element",
        },
        {
          "textContent": ".  In the future, the IAB expects that a major portion of the
   Internet will support both TCP/IP and OSI (inter-)network protocols
   in parallel, and it will then be possible to run OSI applications
   across the Internet using full OSI protocol "stacks".









",
          "type": "Text",
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "class": "grey",
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "Internet Architecture Board Standards Track                    [Page 35]",
              "type": "Text",
            },
          ],
          "nodeName": "span",
          "type": "Element",
        },
      ],
      "nodeName": "pre",
      "type": "Element",
    },
    {
      "textContent": "
",
      "type": "Text",
    },
    {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "noprint",
      },
      "children": [],
      "nodeName": "hr",
      "type": "Element",
    },
